# Offline Protocol SDK for Swift

Offline-first messaging for iOS: BLE mesh and internet relay transports, with
end-to-end encryption (MLS, RFC 9420) applied automatically.

This is the Swift package of the
[Offline Protocol SDK](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk).
It needs no React Native.

## Install

In Xcode, add the package by URL and pick a version. In a manifest:

```swift
dependencies: [
    .package(url: "https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-swift.git", from: "@@VERSION@@")
],
targets: [
    .target(name: "YourApp", dependencies: [
        .product(name: "OfflineProtocolSDK", package: "offline-protocol-swift")
    ])
]
```

Requirements: iOS @@IOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET@@ or later. iOS only: the binary
has no macOS or Mac Catalyst slice.

Integrators should read the
[Native iOS distribution guide](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk/blob/v@@VERSION@@/docs/ios-native-distribution.md)
on the main repository at the **same `X.Y.Z` as this package** (see `VERSION`)
before wiring transports and permissions.

## What is in it

| Part | What it is |
|------|------------|
| `offline_protocolFFI` | The Rust library, as an XCFramework for device and simulator |
| Generated bindings | `OfflineProtocol`, `ProtocolConfig`, `MeshServices`, `DataStore` and the rest of the API, generated from the Rust interface |
| Transports | The BLE, internet relay, Wi-Fi Direct, Nostr and Reticulum managers, the same sources the React Native module compiles |
| Storage | The Keychain store for MLS material and the file store for protocol state, opened per account by `OfflineProtocolReferenceStorage` |
| Integrator helpers | `OfflineProtocolReferenceStorage`, `StorageConformance`, `OfflineProtocolDevelopmentCleartextConfig`, `OfflineProtocolEncryptFailedDiagnostics` |

The Rust core is an I/O-free engine. It queues, routes and encrypts, and never
opens a socket or touches a radio for the protocol. A transport manager does
the I/O for one transport: it drains the engine's outbound queue, sends, and
hands inbound bytes back.

## Getting started

1. `import OfflineProtocolSDK`
2. Build `ProtocolConfig` (see [configuration](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk/blob/v@@VERSION@@/docs/configuration.md) on the main repo)
3. `let engine = try OfflineProtocol(config: config)`
4. `engine.setEventCallback(callback: …)` **before** `start()`
5. `let adoption = try OfflineProtocolReferenceStorage.initializeMls(on: engine, appId: config.appId, profile: config.profile)`
   opens the built-in stores. Report `adoption.warning` when it is set: it is
   the only signal that this account started from a fresh MLS identity, with
   an empty outbox and an empty block list.
6. `try engine.start()`, then wire transports (see the integration guide on the main repo)

Upgrading an app that already initializes MLS with its own storage providers
or `initializeMlsWithFileStores` (every app on 0.33.0 and earlier)? Keep them.
The reference storage holds nothing for those accounts, so switching starts a
fresh identity, and `adoption.warning` does not report it (see
[UPGRADING §12.3](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk/blob/v@@VERSION@@/docs/UPGRADING.md#123-native-ios-bump-the-swift-package-not-a-hand-built-xcframework)).

A custom protocol-state backend is verified once, before you wire it in: run
`StorageConformance.run(on:)` against a scratch instance, off the main thread
(the suite blocks on writes of several megabytes). Green is what makes a
backend supported (see the
[distribution guide](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk/blob/v@@VERSION@@/docs/ios-native-distribution.md)).
Lab-only cleartext and `OfflineProtocolDevelopmentCleartextConfig` are in
[native development cleartext](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk/blob/v@@VERSION@@/docs/native-development-cleartext.md).

Transports, plist entries, and background modes are still application wiring;
the package ships the managers, not a drop-in mesh UI.

## Where this package comes from

This repository is generated. Every source in it is a copy of a file in
[offline-protocol-sdk](https://github.com/Offline-Protocol/offline-protocol-sdk),
written here by that repository's release workflow, and the `VERSION` file
names the commit. Changes made here are overwritten by the next release.
Issues and pull requests belong in the main repository.

